INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('North Vietnam 1: Hanoi, the Old Capital', 'Hanoi is the capital of Vietnam. It is a very old city. It is more than one thousand years old. In the year 1010, King Ly Thai To moved the capital here. He called the city Thang Long. Thang Long means "Rising Dragon".

In the center of Hanoi, there is a beautiful lake. Its name is Hoan Kiem Lake. Hoan Kiem means "Returned Sword". There is a famous story about this lake. A long time ago, King Le Loi got a magic sword. He used the sword to fight enemies and he won. After the war, he went on a boat on the lake. A big golden turtle came up from the water. It took the sword back to the Dragon King. Since then, people call it the Lake of the Returned Sword.

Every morning, many people go to the lake. Some people walk or run. Old people do exercises together. Young people dance or play badminton. In the evening, families and friends sit near the water and talk.

Next to the lake, there is the Old Quarter. It has many small and busy streets. In the past, each street sold one kind of thing. For example, Hang Bac Street sold silver, and Hang Ma Street sold paper things. Many streets still have these old names today.

Hanoi is also famous for egg coffee. A man named Nguyen Van Giang made the first egg coffee in the 1940s. At that time, milk was hard to find, so he used egg yolks. Egg coffee is sweet and creamy. Many tourists love it.

If you visit Hanoi, you will see thousands of motorbikes. The streets are loud and busy, but the city is friendly. You will never feel bored in Hanoi.', 'all');
INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('North Vietnam 2: Ha Long Bay', 'Ha Long Bay is in Quang Ninh Province, in the northeast of Vietnam. It is about three or four hours from Hanoi by car or bus. It is one of the most famous places in Vietnam.

Ha Long Bay has about 1,600 islands. Most of them are made of limestone. They are tall and green, and they rise out of the blue-green water. Most of the islands are empty. Nobody lives on them. In 1994, UNESCO made Ha Long Bay a World Heritage Site.

The name Ha Long means "where the dragon comes down". There is an old legend about it. A long time ago, enemies came to Vietnam by sea. The gods sent a family of dragons to help the people. The dragons came down from the sky. They spat out jewels and jade into the sea. The jewels turned into islands, and the islands made a wall against the enemy ships. After that, the dragons stayed and lived in the bay.

Today, many tourists visit Ha Long Bay. Most of them take a boat trip. Some people stay on the boat for one or two nights. They sleep on the boat, eat fresh seafood, and watch the sunset. Many visitors also go kayaking between the islands.

There are also big caves in the bay. The most famous one is Sung Sot Cave. Sung Sot means "Surprise". The cave is very large, and it has strange and beautiful rocks inside.

In the past, many fishermen lived in floating villages on the water. Their houses were on boats or rafts. Today, some fishing families still live there.

If you go to Ha Long Bay, you will need a good camera. Every view is like a picture.', 'all');
INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('North Vietnam 3: Sapa and the Mountain People', 'Sapa is a small town in the mountains of Lao Cai Province. It is in the far north of Vietnam, near China. In the early 1900s, the French built houses there, because the weather was cool in summer.

Sapa is famous for its rice terraces. The farmers cut the sides of the mountains into big steps. Then they plant rice on each step. In spring, the terraces are full of water and they shine like mirrors. In summer, they are bright green. In September, the rice is ready, and the terraces turn yellow and gold.

Near Sapa, there is Fansipan Mountain. It is more than 3,100 meters high. It is the highest mountain in Vietnam, and people call it "the Roof of Indochina". In the past, climbers needed two or three days to get to the top. In 2016, a cable car opened. Now visitors can go up in about fifteen or twenty minutes.

Many ethnic minority groups live around Sapa. The two biggest groups are the H''Mong and the Dao. The Black H''Mong people wear dark blue clothes. They make the color with a plant called indigo, so their hands are often blue. The Red Dao women wear a big red scarf on their heads. Many women make beautiful clothes and bags by hand and sell them at the market.

Winter in Sapa is cold. Sometimes it even snows. In summer, the weather is cool and pleasant.

Many tourists go trekking in Sapa. They walk from village to village for one or two days. Sometimes they sleep in a family''s house. This is called a homestay. They eat dinner with the family and learn about their life. Tomorrow, maybe you will go there too!', 'all');
INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('North Vietnam 4: Ninh Binh, Ha Long Bay on Land', 'Ninh Binh is a province about two hours south of Hanoi. People often call it "Ha Long Bay on land". Why? Because it has the same kind of tall limestone mountains, but here they stand in rice fields and rivers, not in the sea.

The most famous place in Ninh Binh is Trang An. In 2014, UNESCO made it a World Heritage Site. At Trang An, visitors sit in a small boat. A local woman or man rows the boat along the river. The boat goes through dark caves under the mountains. The trip takes two or three hours. It is quiet and peaceful.

Another famous place is Tam Coc. Tam Coc means "three caves". Here, many rowers use their feet, not their hands, to row the boat. Tourists are always surprised when they see this!

Ninh Binh is also important in history. In the 10th century, Hoa Lu was the capital of Vietnam. King Dinh Tien Hoang lived and ruled there. Today, you can still visit the old temples of the kings.

If you like walking, you can climb to the top of Mua Cave mountain. There are about five hundred steps. It is hard work, and you will be tired. But at the top, you will see the river, the rice fields, and the mountains. It is one of the best views in Vietnam. There is also a big stone dragon on the top.

Many tourists rent a bicycle in Ninh Binh. They ride slowly along small roads between the rice fields. They see farmers, buffaloes, ducks, and small villages.

Ninh Binh is close to Hanoi, so you can visit it in one day. But many people stay for two or three days, because it is so beautiful and relaxing.', 'all');
INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('North Vietnam 5: Food in the North', 'Vietnamese food is famous around the world, and many famous dishes come from the north.

The most famous dish is pho. Pho is a noodle soup with beef or chicken. Many people believe that pho started in northern Vietnam, near Hanoi and Nam Dinh, about one hundred years ago. The soup cooks for many hours with bones, onion, ginger, and spices. In Hanoi, people usually eat pho for breakfast. Hanoi pho is simple. People don''t add many herbs or sauces to it.

Another famous Hanoi dish is bun cha. Bun cha has grilled pork, rice noodles, fresh herbs, and a sweet and sour sauce. People usually eat it for lunch. In 2016, President Barack Obama visited Hanoi. He ate bun cha with the famous chef Anthony Bourdain in a small, simple restaurant. They sat on small plastic stools, like everyone else. After that, the restaurant became very famous.

Cha ca is a fish dish from Hanoi. The fish is yellow because of turmeric. The cook brings a hot pan to your table, and you add dill and green onions. Then you eat it with noodles and peanuts. There is even a street called Cha Ca Street in the Old Quarter.

In the north, street food is a big part of life. People sit on small plastic chairs on the sidewalk. They eat, drink iced tea, and talk with friends. The iced tea is called tra da. It is very cheap.

Northern food is usually less sweet than food from the south. People in the north like balanced and simple flavors.

When you visit the north, you should try many dishes. Don''t be shy! The best food is often in small places on the street, not in big restaurants.', 'all');
INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('North Vietnam 6: Tet in the North', 'Tet is the Lunar New Year. It is the biggest and most important holiday in Vietnam. It usually comes in late January or in February. Before Tet, people clean their houses, buy new clothes, and go back to their hometowns to see their families.

In the north, every family buys a peach blossom tree or branch. Its flowers are pink. In the south, people usually buy a yellow apricot blossom tree. People believe that these flowers bring luck for the new year. Many families in the north also buy a kumquat tree with small orange fruits.

The most important food of Tet in the north is banh chung. Banh chung is a square cake. It has sticky rice, green beans, and pork inside, and green leaves outside. The family cooks it for about ten hours. In the past, children stayed up at night and watched the fire with their parents and grandparents.

There is an old story about banh chung. Thousands of years ago, a Hung King wanted to choose the next king. He asked his sons to bring him the best food. One son, Lang Lieu, was poor. He made a square rice cake for the Earth and a round rice cake for the Sky. The King loved the cakes, and Lang Lieu became the next king.

During Tet, adults give lucky money to children. The money is in small red envelopes. This is called li xi.

People also believe that the first visitor of the new year is very important. If a happy and successful person comes to your house first, your family will have a good year.

Tet is a time for family, food, and hope. Next year, maybe you will celebrate Tet in Vietnam too!', 'all');
INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('North Vietnam 7: Linh''s Trip to the North', 'My name is Linh. I am twenty years old, and I live in Ho Chi Minh City. Last month, I traveled to the north of Vietnam for the first time. It was a wonderful trip.

First, I flew to Hanoi. The weather was cold, so I bought a warm jacket. On the first morning, I walked around Hoan Kiem Lake. I saw many old people doing exercises. Then I ate a bowl of pho for breakfast. It was delicious, but it was different from the pho in my city. In the afternoon, I drank egg coffee in a small café in the Old Quarter. It was sweet, like a dessert.

On the third day, I took a bus to Ha Long Bay. I stayed on a boat for one night. I went kayaking, and I visited Sung Sot Cave. In the evening, I watched the sunset and ate fresh seafood with other tourists. I met a girl from Australia, and we practiced English together.

After that, I took a night bus to Sapa. It was very cold there. I walked through the rice terraces with a H''Mong guide. Her name was Mai. She was only eighteen, but she spoke English very well. She learned English from tourists! I stayed in her family''s house for one night. Her mother cooked dinner for me, and her little brother showed me his chickens.

The trip was ten days long. I was tired at the end, but I was very happy. I learned a lot about my own country.

Next year, I will go to the north again. I will visit Ninh Binh, and I will climb Fansipan Mountain. I will also speak more English, because I want to make friends from around the world.', 'all');
INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('Vietnam: Bác Hồ (The Story of Ho Chi Minh)', 'Ho Chi Minh is the most famous person in the history of modern Vietnam. Vietnamese people lovingly call him Bác Hồ. In English, this means "Uncle Ho".

Ho Chi Minh was born on May 19, 1890, in Kim Lien village, in Nghe An Province. His name at birth was Nguyen Sinh Cung. Later, his father changed his name to Nguyen Tat Thanh. His father was a teacher, and his family was poor. At that time, France ruled Vietnam, and life was very hard for many Vietnamese people.

In 1911, when he was twenty-one years old, he left Vietnam. He worked as a kitchen helper on a French ship. He wanted to see the world and find a way to help his country. For many years, he lived and worked in many countries, such as France, England, and the United States. He did many different jobs. He washed dishes, he took photos, and he wrote for newspapers. During this time, he used the name Nguyen Ai Quoc, which means "Nguyen who loves his country".

In 1941, after thirty years away, he came back to Vietnam. He lived in a small cave at Pac Bo, in Cao Bang Province, near the border with China. Life there was simple and difficult. He ate simple food, worked at a small stone table, and wrote and planned every day.

On September 2, 1945, Ho Chi Minh stood in front of a huge crowd at Ba Dinh Square in Hanoi. He read the Declaration of Independence. On that day, Vietnam became an independent country, and he became its first President. Today, September 2 is Vietnam''s National Day.

Bác Hồ lived a very simple life. He never married. He lived in a small wooden house on stilts, with only a few things inside. He loved children very much, and children loved him too. He often said that children are the future of the country. One of his most famous sayings is: "Nothing is more precious than independence and freedom."

Ho Chi Minh died on September 2, 1969, in Hanoi. He was seventy-nine years old. Today, many people visit his mausoleum at Ba Dinh Square. They also visit his stilt house nearby. Vietnamese children learn about his life at school, and his picture is in schools and offices all over the country.

When you visit Hanoi, you will see his name in many places. Even the biggest city in the south, Saigon, has his name now. It is called Ho Chi Minh City.', 'all');
INSERT INTO texts (title, body, visibility) VALUES ('World: Different Cultures Around the World', 'There are almost two hundred countries in the world, and every country has its own culture. People eat different food, wear different clothes, and have different customs. Something polite in one country can be strange in another country. Let''s travel around the world and learn about some interesting customs.

Our first stop is Japan. In Japan, people bow when they say hello. A small bow is for friends, and a deep bow shows more respect. Japanese people also take off their shoes before they go into a house, just like many people in Vietnam. And here is a surprise: when Japanese people eat noodles, they often make a loud slurping sound. This is not rude. It shows that the food is delicious!

Next, we go to India. Many people in India say "Namaste". They put their hands together in front of their chest and bow a little. In many Indian families, people eat with their right hand, not with a fork or chopsticks. Every autumn, Indians celebrate Diwali, the Festival of Lights. People light small lamps, set off fireworks, and give sweets to their friends and neighbors.

Now let''s fly to New Zealand. The Maori are the first people of New Zealand. They have a special greeting called the hongi. Two people press their noses and foreheads together. The Maori believe that they share the "breath of life" in this way.

Our next stop is Mexico. Every year, on November 1 and 2, Mexicans celebrate the Day of the Dead. But it is not a sad day. Families remember the people in their family who died. They make colorful tables with photos, candles, orange flowers, and the favorite food of the dead person. They also eat sweets in the shape of skulls. In a way, it is a little like when Vietnamese families remember their ancestors.

Then we go to Spain. In Spain, people eat dinner very late, often at nine or ten o''clock in the evening. Restaurants are almost empty at six o''clock, but they are full at eleven!

Our last stop is Russia. If you visit a Russian friend, you can bring flowers. But be careful! You should always bring an odd number of flowers, like three, five, or seven. Russians bring an even number of flowers, like two or four, only to funerals.

As you can see, the world is full of different customs. When you travel, you will meet people with different ideas. Try to learn about their culture before you go. Be open, be curious, and be respectful. Then you will make friends everywhere.', 'all');
